edk2-spacemit — source layout and re-vendoring
==============================================

The upstream sources are vendored in-tree.  `dpkg-source -x` on the source
package yields a tree that builds as-is with `dpkg-buildpackage`; no submodule
initialisation and no network access are required.

Layout
------

  edk2/            EDK2 core, from uefi/edk2, branch k3-release
  edk2-platforms/  platform packages, from uefi/edk2-platforms, branch k3-release
  debian/          packaging

Both directories are plain tracked directories, not git submodules.  The
nested components of edk2 (openssl, mbedtls, brotli, libspdm, googletest,
oniguruma, jansson, mipisyst, cmocka, libfdt, subhook) and of edk2-platforms
(opensbi) are flattened in the same way.

The exact upstream revision currently vendored is recorded in the commit
message of the "vendor:" commits.  There is no other in-tree revision record,
so keep those messages accurate.

debian/rules sets `WORKSPACE = $(CURDIR)`, i.e. the source root, and
`PACKAGES_PATH = $(WORKSPACE)/edk2:$(WORKSPACE)/edk2-platforms`.  This is why
the two directories must keep their names and sit at the top level.

Re-vendoring
------------

Use the `vendor.sh` script at the top level:

  ./vendor.sh <component> [ref] [url]

  component : edk2 | edk2-platforms
  ref       : branch, tag or commit id (default: k3-release)

The script clones the upstream repository to a temporary directory, syncs it
over the corresponding directory with `rsync --delete`, strips `.git` and
`.gitmodules`, and stages the result.  It does not commit — review the diff and
commit yourself, recording the new upstream SHA in the commit message.

The upstream URL has no built-in default, so that no particular upstream host
is baked into this repository.  Supply it as the third argument or through the
environment:

  ./vendor.sh edk2 k3-release <url>
  EDK2_VENDOR_URL=<url> ./vendor.sh edk2
  EDK2_PLATFORMS_VENDOR_URL=<url> ./vendor.sh edk2-platforms

Note for anyone re-vendoring: a plain `git add` is not enough.  Upstream
tracks some files that also match a `.gitignore` rule of the component they
live in (for example mbedtls/library/error.c and
openssl/include/openssl/asn1_mac.h).  Such rules only affect untracked files,
so they are inert upstream, but a fresh import has no tracking history to go
by and `git add -A` drops those files silently.  vendor.sh re-adds every path
listed in the upstream index instead.

Do not remove the `.gitignore` rules in this repository's root: the Bianbu CI
packaging tool refuses to run on a working tree with untracked files, and
in-tree EDK2 builds leave Build/, Conf/, fitimage/, edk2.itb and
debian/.debhelper/ behind.

Note that the build also rewrites two *tracked* files: the
dh_update_autotools_config helper copies the system config.guess and config.sub
over the ones under edk2/MdeModulePkg/Universal/RegularExpressionDxe/oniguruma/.
Tracked files cannot be ignored, but `debian/rules clean` restores them, so run
it before invoking the packaging tool on a tree that has been built in place.

The RISC-V ACPI modules are linked with BaseTools/Scripts/GccRiscVAcpi.lds,
whose /DISCARD/ list matches `.got.plt`.  binutils 2.38 rejects that with
"discarded output section: .got.plt"; the script was written for binutils 2.39
and later, so build with a toolchain of that vintage or newer.

